  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "The learner appears to be **holistic**. I submitted that verdict with confidence 0.88, and the session is now over.\n\n**Design:** I trained the learner on two tasks, `L1-S1-M1` and `L4-S2-M3`. Then I tested tasks where the two hypotheses predict opposite costs:\n\n| Test task | New rules | Highest overlap with a trained task | Part-based predicts | Holistic predicts | Mean examples to criterion |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `L4-S1-M1` | 0 | 0 | cheapest | most costly | ~39 (11 tests) |\n| `L1-S3-M1`, `L1-S4-M1` | 1 | 0.625 | in between | cheapest | ~20 (12 tests) |\n| `L3-S3-M4` | 3 | \u22640.19 | most costly | in between | ~31 (7 tests) |\n\n**Result:** The task built only from already-learned rules was the hardest, and the tasks with a new rule but similar input-output mappings were the easiest. So the cost of learning followed how similar the mappings were, not how many rules were new. That is the opposite of what the part-based learner would show.\n\n**Caveat:** The tests are very noisy; single results for the same task ranged from 1 to 61. The key gap (about 39 vs. 20 examples) is roughly 3 standard errors, so it's fairly solid but not decisive, which is why I gave 0.88 rather than higher. I used 32 of the 60 budget.",
